# Crossbar in public mode: the public hostname is the way in, and the tailnet is not one.
#
# This runs before the server on every start, which is what makes a mode switch one action.
# The switch rewrites the mode in `.env` and leaves; systemd restarts crossbar; crossbar
# wants this unit and the private one, and the check below means exactly one of them does
# anything. Nothing has to be remembered, and nothing outside the file has to be kept in
# step with it.
#
# A switch opens the new door and closes the old one later, on a timer, instead of closing
# it at once. Closing it first -- what this unit used to do -- is destructive for every
# phone: the app only learns that the server moved by reading `/api/health`, and it can only
# ask after authenticating. A door closed before the app has read the answer is a device
# that has to be re-enrolled with a hand-issued code. With the overlap the app polls the
# address it already knows, finds an `origin` that differs from the one it stored, adopts
# that origin, and keeps its device key. That is the whole reason the window exists.
#
# The overlap is not a hole, because the trust posture follows the mode and not the door: in
# public mode the identity header is not believed at all, so the tailnet door only admits
# devices that hold keys. Both directions of that argument are measured on the rehearsal
# host before a deploy, not inferred here.

[Unit]
Description=Crossbar in public mode (the public hostname in, the tailnet out)

After=network-online.target
Wants=network-online.target

Before=crossbar.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# `RemainAfterExit` is deliberately absent, and that absence is load-bearing. A oneshot
# without it goes inactive the moment it has run, which is the whole mechanism of a switch:
# the units are not active afterwards, so the next `systemctl restart crossbar` starts them
# again and the shape is re-applied every time. `RemainAfterExit=yes` would leave this one
# "active (exited)" after its first run; every later restart would find it already active,
# skip it, and leave the box in the previous mode while reporting a clean restart. If you
# are reading this because you are about to add it: don't.
EnvironmentFile=/home/admin/crossbar/.env

# How long a switch leaves the outgoing door open, in seconds, and the floor under it. The
# value in `.env` wins over this line: at spawn the environment is merged as
# `strv_env_merge(..., context->environment, params->files_env)` and later entries override,
# so the file's variables are applied last -- not because of which directive comes first
# here. Measured in systemd v252 (`src/core/execute.c`) and v257 (`src/core/exec-invoke.c`);
# the order is the same in both. 900 is fifteen minutes.
Environment=CROSSBAR_SWITCH_GRACE_SECONDS=900

# Which mode this unit is for is answered by the same file the server reads, so there is one
# answer to "which mode is this deployment in" and no second copy to drift.
#
# `grep` here rather than `ConditionEnvironment=`, which looks like the obvious tool and is
# not: that condition is checked against the *manager's* environment (/proc/1/environ), so
# `EnvironmentFile=` never reaches it and the check fails in both modes. Measured -- both
# units were skipped, including the one whose mode was in force -- and a condition that
# fails quietly is worse than no condition, because the box looks switched and is not.
#
# `-x` is a whole-line match, so a comment or a longer name that merely contains this one
# cannot satisfy it.
ExecCondition=/usr/bin/grep -qx CROSSBAR_NETWORK_MODE=public /home/admin/crossbar/.env

# The public route comes back first, so the new way in exists before the old one goes.
#
# A command rather than `Wants=caddy.service`, and `crossbar-private.service` is written the
# same way for the opposite direction. A dependency is resolved while the start is still
# being planned, before conditions are read, so a `Conflicts=` on the private unit stopped
# Caddy even on a start whose own condition then declined to make it -- a public outage
# decided by a check nobody reads. A command here runs only if the check passed, which is
# the property the switch rests on. Measured, not assumed, with a stand-in unit.
ExecStart=/usr/bin/systemctl start caddy

# Then the tailnet door is closed -- but after the window, not now, and by a transient unit
# so there is nothing to clean up and no fifth unit file to keep in step.
#
# The window is `CROSSBAR_SWITCH_GRACE_SECONDS`, set in `.env` or falling back to the 900
# above. `--on-active=` takes a bare number as seconds, which is what the name says -- do
# not append an `s`, or a value of `15min` would arrive as `15mins` and be refused.
#
# The `grep` in the middle is the important half, and it is the same whole-line check as the
# condition above, for the same reason: a switch that is switched back inside the window
# must not have the earlier switch's timer close the door that is now wanted. The close
# reads the mode in force when it fires, not the mode that scheduled it -- which is also why
# a switch back inside the window needs no second timer.
#
# `-` because that second timer cannot be created: a switch back inside the window finds
# `crossbar-grace-public.timer` still pending, systemd-run refuses a name that already
# exists, and the pending timer is the one that does the right thing anyway. `--collect`
# unloads the unit once it has fired, so a refusal is not a queue that grows.
#
# `tailscale serve --https=443 off` can still fail with "handler does not exist" when
# nothing was serving. That is the state the command asks for and is tolerated, exactly as
# when it was an `ExecStart=` here -- it is now the transient unit's exit status, so it
# leaves a failed `crossbar-grace-public.service` line in the journal rather than a failed
# shaper.
#
# `/bin/sh -c` for one reason only: systemd expands `${NAME}` on a command line but has no
# `${NAME:-default}`. That operator is enabled only while an `EnvironmentFile=` is being
# read (`src/basic/env-file.c`'s `merge_env_file_push` is the only caller of
# `replace_env_full` that passes REPLACE_ENV_ALLOW_EXTENDED; inline `Environment=` expands
# specifiers but no variables at all) and is not enabled for `Exec*=`: the command-line path
# calls `replace_env_full(..., flags = 0, ...)` and the `:` branch, commented "unsupported
# syntax, i.e. do no replacement", hands the text through unchanged. A line written as
# `--on-active=${CROSSBAR_SWITCH_GRACE_SECONDS:-900}` would therefore reach systemd-run as a
# literal time span and be refused, and `-` would swallow it into a switch that never closes
# the old door. Measured across systemd v252 to v262 and current main; no version expands it
# there. The shell, which does have the operator and does have the EnvironmentFile's
# variables, is the right place for the fallback.
#
# The 900 is written in both places on purpose, and they cover different cases: the
# `Environment=` line above is the value the unit states when the file says nothing, and the
# shell's operator is what keeps a file that says nothing *by writing an empty line* --
# `CROSSBAR_SWITCH_GRACE_SECONDS=` -- from expanding to `--on-active=`, which systemd-run
# refuses and `-` would then swallow into a switch that never closes the old door.
ExecStart=-/bin/sh -c '/usr/bin/systemd-run --collect --unit=crossbar-grace-public --on-active="${CROSSBAR_SWITCH_GRACE_SECONDS:-900}" /bin/sh -c "/usr/bin/grep -qx CROSSBAR_NETWORK_MODE=public /home/admin/crossbar/.env && exec /usr/bin/tailscale serve --https=443 off"'

# Then the relay, if this box runs one.
#
# coturn's whole view of who it is comes from `.env`, and only at its start: `ExecStartPre`
# renders the template with `envsubst`, taking `realm` from `CROSSBAR_PUBLIC_HOSTNAME`
# (`deploy/coturn.conf:27`) and `external-ip` from `CROSSBAR_TURN_EXTERNAL_IP`. The switch
# rewrites `.env`, so a relay left running across one keeps advertising the mode the box
# just left -- and coturn starts happily on a stale value, so it shows up as media that will
# not relay rather than as a configuration error, which is the expensive way to find out.
# Restarting it here is what makes the relay part of the same switch instead of a second
# thing an operator has to remember, and the next reboot would not fix it either.
#
# `try-restart` rather than `restart`: it acts only on a unit that is already running, so a
# deployment with no relay configured is left alone rather than being pulled in and started
# by a mode switch. `-` because this is best-effort even so: a box where the unit does not
# exist answers systemctl with an error rather than the no-op, and a shaper that has already
# moved the front door must not report itself failed over the relay it was never asked to
# have.
ExecStart=-/usr/bin/systemctl try-restart crossbar-turn.service
